1
00:00:05,094 --> 00:00:11,550
It's an honor to be here today.

2
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:19,992
My name is JungMin Ahn.

3
00:00:19,992 --> 00:00:27,400
I'm a senior software engineer at KakaoBank.

4
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:30,949
Today, I'd like to share what we learned from

5
00:00:30,949 --> 00:00:34,420
operating a large-scale modular iOS project.

6
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:37,821
I'll focus on practical lessons about developer experience

7
00:00:37,821 --> 00:00:40,321
architecture and project operations.

8
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:45,400
First, let me briefly introduce KakaoBank.

9
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,567
KakaoBank is Korea's
mobile-first digital bank.

10
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:51,567
We launched in 2017.

11
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:59,980
Today, we have 27 million customers
and 20 million monthly active users.

12
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:03,670
We also have more than
100 mobile developers.

13
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:09,640
I have been an iOS developer for thirteen years.

14
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:15,200
I joined KakaoBank in 2016,
before the official launch.

15
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:21,220
So, I have worked on this iOS
project from the very beginning.

16
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:27,780
I have seen it grow from a small
project into a very large one.

17
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:32,631
Today, I'd like to share what
we learned during that journey.

18
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:39,220
Let me start with the
journey of our iOS project.

19
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:42,265
This journey explains why
we chose modularization.

20
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:51,020
When we started in 2016, the project
had about seven hundred files.

21
00:01:51,020 --> 00:01:54,600
At that time, the
codebase was still small.

22
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:59,300
But the service grew very quickly.

23
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:03,650
By the end of 2020, the
project had seven thousand

24
00:02:03,650 --> 00:02:08,260
files and about seven hundred
thousand lines of code.

25
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:14,141
During that period, our architecture
also evolved from MVVM to RIBs.

26
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:20,420
And the project continued to grow.

27
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:25,685
By June 2026, it had reached about fifty

28
00:02:25,685 --> 00:02:29,815
thousand files and five
million lines of code.

29
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:36,129
This was far beyond the scale
of our original project.

30
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:42,046
As the project grew, two
major problems became clear.

31
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:47,582
First, unrestricted code access
increased coupling between features.

32
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:53,004
A small change in one place
could affect many other areas.

33
00:02:55,500 --> 00:03:02,205
Second, builds became slower
and failed more often.

34
00:03:02,940 --> 00:03:06,761
This made everyday development
much more difficult.

35
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:13,000
These problems were part
of a repeating cycle.

36
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:15,724
Let me show you how this cycle worked.

37
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:21,607
As the service grew, we had
to develop new products.

38
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:27,173
At the same time, we also had to
maintain all of our existing products.

39
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:33,980
To handle more work, the
development teams also grew.

40
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:39,248
But even with more developers, we
often had a shortage of resources.

41
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:46,005
More developers produced more
code, and without clear boundaries.

42
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:50,254
Dependencies between
features also increased.

43
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:56,509
As the code and dependencies
grew, build time also increased.

44
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:02,850
Developers spent more time
waiting and less time developing.

45
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:10,757
Maintenance cost increased, working hours
increased, and tests became hard to write.

46
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:15,286
So, the cost of every
change became higher.

47
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:19,762
Still, the team completed the development.

48
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:24,834
New products were released,
and the product grew again.

49
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:29,112
Then, the same cycle started again.

50
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:31,835
The team continued to deliver.

51
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:35,993
But each cycle became
slower and more expensive.

52
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:40,318
So, we ask ourselves a question.

53
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:44,590
Could modular development
help us break this cycle?

54
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:49,035
This was the beginning of
our modularization journey.

55
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:56,599
We started modularization with
only three modules in 2017.

56
00:04:57,220 --> 00:04:58,826
Then the number grew quickly.

57
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:04,517
By the end of 2021, we
had forty-nine modules.

58
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:09,814
As the number of modules grew,

59
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:13,267
manual management was no longer practical.

60
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:16,770
We needed tooling for
complex project settings.

61
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:21,682
We also needed templates to
create projects with shared rules.

62
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:26,572
And the number kept growing.

63
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:33,401
By June 2026, our project had more than
one thousand three hundred modules.

64
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:38,875
At this scale, modularization itself
also needs careful management.

65
00:05:41,910 --> 00:05:44,780
The business cycle was still the same.

66
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:49,774
We continued to develop new
products and maintain existing ones.

67
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:55,650
The team continues to
grow as the project grows.

68
00:05:57,900 --> 00:06:01,906
And of course, more developers
still produce more code.

69
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:06,914
But now, our development
approach was different.

70
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:14,275
Instead of working in one large project,
teams could work in separate modules.

71
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:17,888
This gave us several benefits.

72
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:23,413
Incremental builds became faster.

73
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,766
Teams could develop features
with their own demo apps.

74
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:34,001
Testing became faster and
changes had fewer side effects.

75
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:41,094
This helped teams complete development
with a faster feedback cycle.

76
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:44,176
Then, the project continues to grow.

77
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:53,539
The cycle did not disappear, but the
development path became more efficient.

78
00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:59,020
Project growth leads to teams growth.

79
00:06:59,020 --> 00:07:01,620
Teams growth leads to module growth.

80
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:06,540
And module-based development helps
us deliver features independently.

81
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:15,400
For developers, the two
biggest improvements are clear.

82
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:20,605
First, we could develop
features independently.

83
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:25,183
Second, we could build and
test changes much faster.

84
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:32,329
Here is one build time
comparison on M3 Max.

85
00:07:33,040 --> 00:07:36,320
A clean build over the main app took about

86
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:39,040
two thousand seconds.

87
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:42,600
An incremental build still
took about forty seconds.

88
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:47,338
But a feature module's incremental
build took only three seconds.

89
00:07:48,040 --> 00:07:50,529
The UI module also took
about three seconds.

90
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:55,440
Here are a few of our demo apps.

91
00:07:55,440 --> 00:08:00,972
In action, each demo app focuses
on a specific feature or UI module.

92
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:06,240
Developers can quickly learn it,
check the result, and continue

93
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:07,560
working.

94
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:10,147
This makes the feedback loop much faster.

95
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:17,133
The build time improvement is significant.

96
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:23,473
But faster build is not our final goal.

97
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:27,096
Our real goal is better
developer experience.

98
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:32,085
Let's look at one feature in the app.

99
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:35,840
At a high level,

100
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:39,797
this feature depends on
two areas, UI and core.

101
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:46,818
We separated this responsibility
into clear architectural areas.

102
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:53,079
The UI areas include reusable components,
UI helpers, pop-ups, and resources.

103
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:59,288
The core areas include networking,
authentication, security, and logging.

104
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:05,100
Instead of keeping everything in one large

105
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:10,623
codebase, we divide each responsibility
into an independent module.

106
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:15,940
This makes our feedback loop much faster.

107
00:09:15,940 --> 00:09:21,860
Developers can edit, build,
learn, and test in a short cycle.

108
00:09:21,860 --> 00:09:25,046
For us, modularization is about dx.

109
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:30,664
But speed alone is not enough.

110
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:34,780
We want faster iteration,
safer changes, and

111
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:36,780
predictable results.

112
00:09:36,780 --> 00:09:40,005
And we want to repeat
this cycle continuously.

113
00:09:43,260 --> 00:09:51,104
This becomes the foundation
of our modular architectures.

114
00:09:53,540 --> 00:09:57,854
As we add more features,
we also add more modules.

115
00:09:58,880 --> 00:10:03,387
This is a natural result
of independent development.

116
00:10:06,432 --> 00:10:08,503
But scale comes with a cost.

117
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:14,619
More modules means more
configuration complexity,

118
00:10:14,619 --> 00:10:16,697
and that created more
operational overhead.

119
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:22,392
We had to manage project
and target manually.

120
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:25,147
Dependencies became more complex.

121
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:28,840
Module structure became inconsistent.

122
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:35,165
We repeat the same demo app
and test setup across many
modules.

123
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:40,260
Resources are duplicated across modules.

124
00:10:40,820 --> 00:10:44,340
Each inconsistency looked small.

125
00:10:44,340 --> 00:10:48,507
But over time, they became
a large operational cost.

126
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:53,832
We need a way to standardize
and automate this work.

127
00:10:56,060 --> 00:11:04,100
Now, let's look at project

128
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:05,940
generation.

129
00:11:05,940 --> 00:11:11,666
Before choosing a tool, we first
define what we need from it.

130
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:20,248
When we create the project
and modules manually,

131
00:11:20,248 --> 00:11:26,300
we also had to manage
their dependencies by hand.

132
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:30,660
This made it easy to make
configuration mistakes.

133
00:11:30,660 --> 00:11:31,900
For example,

134
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:37,797
in the application target
settings, we had to

135
00:11:37,797 --> 00:11:40,468
configure how each framework was embedded.

136
00:11:43,300 --> 00:11:51,916
If this setting was wrong, the
app could fail on runtime, and

137
00:11:51,916 --> 00:11:58,722
checking it manually became difficult
as the number of modules grew.

138
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:07,319
We need to generate different projects
and workspaces for different conditions.

139
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:13,591
Using environment variables,
we could exclude specific

140
00:12:13,591 --> 00:12:18,663
projects or generate a workspace
based on a selected project.

141
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:26,766
This gave developers a focused environment
with only the features they need.

142
00:12:29,980 --> 00:12:35,943
We need to generate and visualize
the complete dependency graph.

143
00:12:36,540 --> 00:12:43,658
When we looked at the whole
structure, we could understand where

144
00:12:43,658 --> 00:12:47,496
a module should belong to which
module it should depend on.

145
00:12:50,220 --> 00:12:54,898
The graph did not design the
architecture for us, but it

146
00:12:54,898 --> 00:13:00,348
gave us information to discuss
and design a more scalable
structure.

147
00:13:03,060 --> 00:13:07,372
At the time, we compared
Tuist with XcodeGen.

148
00:13:08,180 --> 00:13:10,988
Both tools generate Xcode projects.

149
00:13:11,780 --> 00:13:18,962
We chose Tuist because we could define
dynamic configurations using Swift.

150
00:13:20,360 --> 00:13:25,665
And of course, as iOS developers,
we already knew Swift well.

151
00:13:28,940 --> 00:13:32,440
Project generation solved only one part.

152
00:13:32,440 --> 00:13:37,242
That's why we use Tuist to
manage module relationships.

153
00:13:39,940 --> 00:13:46,295
In this example, the app depends
on module A and module B.

154
00:13:46,295 --> 00:13:48,094
Module A also depends on module C.

155
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:56,876
The app embed module A and module
B, and module A links to module C.

156
00:13:58,860 --> 00:14:05,546
If the app does not
embed module C, it fails

157
00:14:05,546 --> 00:14:09,206
at runtime with a
library not loaded error.

158
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:18,681
In the project definitions, the app
directly depends on module A and module B.

159
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:22,774
But because module A
links to module C, the

160
00:14:23,467 --> 00:14:27,560
generated app must embed
all three frameworks,

161
00:14:27,560 --> 00:14:29,942
module A, module B, and module C.

162
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:38,730
In the Tuist definitions,
we only declared the

163
00:14:38,730 --> 00:14:40,454
app's direct dependencies,
module A, module B.

164
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:49,396
The generated project includes the
required framework relationships.

165
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:57,260
Tuist generated the application
project and the project for

166
00:14:57,260 --> 00:15:03,240
module A, module B, and module C.

167
00:15:03,240 --> 00:15:08,426
Tuist generated the application project
and the project for module A, module B.

168
00:15:09,320 --> 00:15:16,174
Because module A depends on
module C, Tuist also generated

169
00:15:16,174 --> 00:15:19,602
the module C project and
added it to the workspace.

170
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:31,720
The generated app now embeds
module A, module B, and module C.

171
00:15:31,720 --> 00:15:37,600
Tuist detects the indirect dependencies
and configures module C automatically.

172
00:15:38,540 --> 00:15:41,637
This prevents missing
frameworks error at runtime.

173
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:48,442
As we added more modules,
we also add demo apps.

174
00:15:49,180 --> 00:15:53,078
Each demo app needs its
own dependency structure.

175
00:15:56,720 --> 00:15:58,835
We use environment variables.

176
00:16:00,620 --> 00:16:04,370
starting with Tuist to
control project generation.

177
00:16:04,940 --> 00:16:09,152
For example, we set Tuist's
exclude-module-B value to true.

178
00:16:10,100 --> 00:16:14,047
The manifest reads these
values through ProcessInfo.

179
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:22,825
It then generates the application
without a dependency on module B.

180
00:16:25,720 --> 00:16:30,816
After generation, the application
contains only this selected module.

181
00:16:33,280 --> 00:16:37,545
Module B is not included
in the generated workspace.

182
00:16:38,220 --> 00:16:45,822
It is also not added to
the application target.

183
00:16:46,740 --> 00:16:52,411
This shows how environment variables can
change project generation dynamically.

184
00:16:55,020 --> 00:17:00,449
Previously, when we generated
the workspace, it included the

185
00:17:01,284 --> 00:17:06,431
application and project for
module A, module B, and module C.

186
00:17:09,140 --> 00:17:15,055
For specific features, we wanted a
workspace with only its required modules.

187
00:17:15,800 --> 00:17:19,672
Developers did not need the whole project.

188
00:17:20,580 --> 00:17:24,506
For example, the module
A demo app required

189
00:17:24,940 --> 00:17:27,160
module A and module C.

190
00:17:27,160 --> 00:17:32,050
So, the workspace included
only those projects.

191
00:17:34,340 --> 00:17:40,029
We can generate a focused project
by selecting the demo app target.

192
00:17:42,420 --> 00:17:50,079
Tuist generates a small workspace with
the demo app and its required modules.

193
00:17:53,060 --> 00:17:58,749
The generated workspace contains only
the module A and module C projects.

194
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:05,604
This gives the module A demo app a focused

195
00:18:05,604 --> 00:18:09,735
development environment
with only the code it needs.

196
00:18:12,220 --> 00:18:16,408
I drew a dependency graph
manually, but in a real

197
00:18:16,408 --> 00:18:19,281
project, we can now
generate and visualize it.

198
00:18:20,340 --> 00:18:28,240
Every time a new module is added,
maintaining it by hand is not practical.

199
00:18:30,780 --> 00:18:33,708
We can also generate a
dependency graph with Tuist.

200
00:18:36,220 --> 00:18:39,217
This graph shows the actual
module relationships.

201
00:18:39,740 --> 00:18:44,613
It helps us verify the structure
and find unexpected dependencies.

202
00:18:47,520 --> 00:18:49,820
Project generation helps us

203
00:18:49,820 --> 00:18:51,759
manage the project structure.

204
00:18:52,820 --> 00:18:57,257
Next, we need to design the
relationship between modules.

205
00:19:00,540 --> 00:19:06,850
Our main rule is to separate the
interface from implementation.

206
00:19:07,880 --> 00:19:12,669
This minimizes the impact
of changes, keeps public

207
00:19:13,960 --> 00:19:16,529
APIs small, and prevents
circular dependencies.

208
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:19,780
Here,

209
00:19:19,780 --> 00:19:30,489
feature A and feature B each
have their own relationship.

210
00:19:32,540 --> 00:19:39,783
If feature A needs feature B, it can
depend directly on feature B

211
00:19:41,940 --> 00:19:46,078
But if feature B also needs feature A,

212
00:19:46,900 --> 00:19:49,459
we create a circular dependency.

213
00:19:51,780 --> 00:19:57,096
To avoid this, we separate each
interface from each implementation.

214
00:19:59,500 --> 00:20:04,105
Each feature can now depend on
the other feature's interface.

215
00:20:05,560 --> 00:20:10,448
Instead of each implementation,
this creates clear boundaries.

216
00:20:12,940 --> 00:20:16,620
The app creates the implementations and

217
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:20,258
injects them into features that need them.

218
00:20:22,980 --> 00:20:26,500
However, this approach has a limitation.

219
00:20:26,500 --> 00:20:29,040
We have to inject every
dependency manually.

220
00:20:29,960 --> 00:20:35,865
As the number of features grows,
this setup becomes hard to manage.

221
00:20:38,460 --> 00:20:42,615
So, we introduce a container
to manage these connections.

222
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:46,080
The app registers

223
00:20:46,080 --> 00:20:48,138
implementation in the containers.

224
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:53,591
Features can then resolve
the interfaces they need.

225
00:20:54,480 --> 00:20:58,513
This reduces repeated
manual dependency setup.

226
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:05,465
When the app starts, we create
the service and the container.

227
00:21:05,960 --> 00:21:11,390
Then, we register each service
and resolve the interface we need.

228
00:21:17,186 --> 00:21:23,841
This works, but we still have to
write every registration manually.

229
00:21:25,480 --> 00:21:28,360
Then, we ask another question.

230
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:34,222
What if we have to write this
code for hundreds of demo apps?

231
00:21:37,160 --> 00:21:42,067
Other platforms already have
similar automatic registration

232
00:21:42,067 --> 00:21:46,060
patterns, such as Spring
Beans and Android Hilt.

233
00:21:47,991 --> 00:21:52,409
So, we developed a lightweight DI
container for iOS applications.

234
00:21:53,300 --> 00:21:58,828
It supported type-safe resolution
and automatic module registration.

235
00:22:02,760 --> 00:22:10,560
In a demo app, one call
automatically registers the modules.

236
00:22:10,560 --> 00:22:14,815
Then, the Inject property wrapper
resolves the service by its key.

237
00:22:15,740 --> 00:22:20,655
This makes the demo app's
setup code much smaller.

238
00:22:21,500 --> 00:22:29,513
Developers can focus on the example they
want to test, instead of dependency setup.

239
00:22:32,520 --> 00:22:36,009
We use automatic scanning
only for demo apps.

240
00:22:36,840 --> 00:22:40,853
In production, we register each
dependency in the container.

241
00:22:42,100 --> 00:22:43,640
And in test,

242
00:22:43,640 --> 00:22:47,452
we verify that every
dependency key was registered.

243
00:22:48,300 --> 00:22:52,270
This helps us catch missing
registration for release.

244
00:22:55,040 --> 00:22:58,755
We also reviewed several
open source approaches.

245
00:22:59,260 --> 00:23:02,030
These references help us shape our design.

246
00:23:05,560 --> 00:23:08,932
Our next challenge was
scaling a large feature

247
00:23:09,283 --> 00:23:11,763
without losing clear module boundaries.

248
00:23:13,904 --> 00:23:17,105
Let's start with one
large feature, feature A.

249
00:23:19,160 --> 00:23:26,329
As feature A grew, it included
many responsibilities,

250
00:23:26,329 --> 00:23:29,685
home detail, management,
payments, and more.

251
00:23:32,280 --> 00:23:37,720
We divided this responsibility
into small modules, but

252
00:23:38,112 --> 00:23:42,514
the modules still need to
share some internal APIs.

253
00:23:44,522 --> 00:23:49,337
For this, we use Swift
package access modifier.

254
00:23:50,600 --> 00:23:54,098
It allows modules in the
same group to share the

255
00:23:54,737 --> 00:23:58,401
API, while hiding those
APIs from other features.

256
00:23:59,680 --> 00:24:02,311
This keeps the public API small.

257
00:24:04,640 --> 00:24:07,859
This is not limited to
Swift package manager.

258
00:24:08,500 --> 00:24:13,086
In a Xcode project, we can
assign the same package name to

259
00:24:13,600 --> 00:24:16,117
multiple targets with a build setting.

260
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:26,864
Feature A and feature A
home belong to the same

261
00:24:26,864 --> 00:24:32,963
package group, so they
can share package APIs.

262
00:24:34,560 --> 00:24:36,586
Feature B is outside this group.

263
00:24:38,260 --> 00:24:43,580
If feature B tries to access
feature A home's package API,

264
00:24:43,580 --> 00:24:48,394
the compiler checks
the package boundaries.

265
00:24:50,960 --> 00:24:57,324
Because feature B is outside the
group, the compiler blocks the access.

266
00:24:58,360 --> 00:25:00,303
The internal API remains hidden.

267
00:25:02,600 --> 00:25:08,681
In these modules, the type initializer
and function all use package access.

268
00:25:09,780 --> 00:25:13,500
They are available only
inside the same package group.

269
00:25:15,672 --> 00:25:21,606
Another module can import is the
module, but if it belongs to a

270
00:25:21,606 --> 00:25:27,720
different package group, it still
cannot access the package API.

271
00:25:30,955 --> 00:25:37,743
For real large features, we assign the
same package name to related modules.

272
00:25:39,040 --> 00:25:41,980
Here the package name is feature A.

273
00:25:43,589 --> 00:25:47,570
We also create an internal
interface module for feature A.

274
00:25:48,120 --> 00:25:52,453
It contains contracts shared
only inside this feature group.

275
00:25:53,460 --> 00:25:56,444
The DI container manages
their implementations.

276
00:25:58,699 --> 00:26:04,418
Feature A registers its internal
implementation in the container.

277
00:26:05,120 --> 00:26:08,248
If some module resolve
the interface they need.

278
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:11,166
Other features cannot access.

279
00:26:12,471 --> 00:26:18,210
These internal package APIs so the feature

280
00:26:18,210 --> 00:26:22,412
can grow without expanding its public API.

281
00:26:25,515 --> 00:26:29,940
Clear boundaries make
our changes safer, but

282
00:26:29,940 --> 00:26:34,044
developers also need
to see results quickly.

283
00:26:36,005 --> 00:26:40,311
Our feedback loop is
still at build and learn.

284
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:46,226
But we can choose a small execution
target such as a demo app or preview.

285
00:26:48,500 --> 00:26:50,299
Let's look at feature A home.

286
00:26:52,200 --> 00:26:57,139
We separated each UI into
a small home UI module.

287
00:26:59,400 --> 00:27:06,401
The feature module has its own demo app,
and the UI module has its own preview.

288
00:27:07,160 --> 00:27:10,063
Each environment has a different purpose.

289
00:27:12,506 --> 00:27:17,834
A demo app shows real-life behavior,
real state, and navigation.

290
00:27:18,860 --> 00:27:23,110
It was useful for validating
the whole feature.

291
00:27:24,080 --> 00:27:31,800
A preview focuses on UI rendering
sample state and single screen.

292
00:27:31,800 --> 00:27:35,038
It is useful for faster UI development.

293
00:27:37,380 --> 00:27:40,580
We also use tools such as InjectionIII,

294
00:27:40,580 --> 00:27:43,820
Lookin, Reveal, and Xcode Previews.

295
00:27:43,820 --> 00:27:49,759
These tools help us build, learn,
and validate UI changes faster.

296
00:27:52,500 --> 00:27:56,546
We build our design system
as reusable UI modules.

297
00:27:57,100 --> 00:28:03,667
Each UI module provides a demo app
with examples of each component.

298
00:28:04,420 --> 00:28:10,460
Developers can validate the components
before integrating them into

299
00:28:10,460 --> 00:28:12,860
a feature.

300
00:28:12,860 --> 00:28:17,314
We applied the same patterns
on different levels,

301
00:28:17,702 --> 00:28:20,566
feature UI, design system,
and individual components.

302
00:28:34,581 --> 00:28:38,625
Resources are also easy to
duplicate across features,

303
00:28:39,140 --> 00:28:44,383
so we move shared resources
into dedicated modules,

304
00:28:44,383 --> 00:28:47,030
and we access them through generated APIs.

305
00:28:48,700 --> 00:28:51,903
This reduces invalid resource
reference at runtime.

306
00:28:54,700 --> 00:28:58,636
Xcode compiles asset catalogs with actool.

307
00:28:59,540 --> 00:29:04,057
As the number of images grew,
asset compilation became slower.

308
00:29:04,860 --> 00:29:08,261
This affects app build,
preview, and demo apps.

309
00:29:09,020 --> 00:29:13,902
So, we split a large asset catalog
into separate resource modules.

310
00:29:16,860 --> 00:29:20,060
Storyboards and XIBs need extra care.

311
00:29:20,060 --> 00:29:24,032
Moving images to another bundle
can break their reference.

312
00:29:25,120 --> 00:29:31,073
We verify the module settings
after moving them, and we load

313
00:29:31,073 --> 00:29:37,820
Storyboards and XIBs in test to
catch runtime issues before release.

314
00:29:40,673 --> 00:29:44,193
When possible, we prefer code-based UI.

315
00:29:44,940 --> 00:29:49,139
It is easier to move across
modules, provides stronger

316
00:29:49,139 --> 00:29:53,260
compile-time checks, and
causes fewer runtime issues.

317
00:30:07,820 --> 00:30:13,341
These feedback environments
are useful, but large-scale

318
00:30:13,341 --> 00:30:16,245
demo apps also create new
operational challenges.

319
00:30:18,260 --> 00:30:21,436
The first challenge is
size of DerivedData.

320
00:30:22,700 --> 00:30:29,039
Each module can have a framework or
demo app and one or more test targetss.

321
00:30:31,340 --> 00:30:37,740
When we build them, Xcode
stores their products

322
00:30:37,740 --> 00:30:39,620
in DerivedData.

323
00:30:39,620 --> 00:30:45,046
Each framework creates a built product
for each configuration and destination.

324
00:30:47,660 --> 00:30:53,445
In this example, module D
uses about 30 megabytes.

325
00:30:55,856 --> 00:30:58,952
One copy of this framework
is not a problem.

326
00:31:00,440 --> 00:31:04,943
But, the unit test targets
also needs to build module D.

327
00:31:07,100 --> 00:31:10,922
If the test uses a demo app as their host,

328
00:31:11,460 --> 00:31:14,764
the demo apps also embedded the framework.

329
00:31:15,820 --> 00:31:19,339
The required number of
built products increases.

330
00:31:21,460 --> 00:31:28,396
When other modules depend on module D,
the build graph becomes even larger.

331
00:31:30,380 --> 00:31:35,822
The same pattern is repeated for every
module's demo apps and test targets.

332
00:31:38,062 --> 00:31:41,728
With many modules, one
test plan can build a large

333
00:31:41,728 --> 00:31:46,117
graph of application, demo
apps, frameworks, and tests.

334
00:31:48,265 --> 00:31:51,350
So, the total size
grows, with both size of

335
00:31:51,350 --> 00:31:55,881
each feature and the
number of feature modules.

336
00:31:56,516 --> 00:32:01,480
For example, if one
feature uses 300 megabytes,

337
00:32:01,480 --> 00:32:07,459
200 modules can create about
60 gigabytes of DerivedData.

338
00:32:08,540 --> 00:32:18,848
At 500 megabytes, the total
can reach 100 gigabytes.

339
00:32:21,191 --> 00:32:25,924
Our solution is to use hostless
unit tests whenever possible.

340
00:32:27,180 --> 00:32:29,020
With a hosted test,

341
00:32:29,020 --> 00:32:33,871
Xcode has to build and launch the
demo app before running the test.

342
00:32:36,320 --> 00:32:41,587
With a hosted test, the test runs
directly against the target framework.

343
00:32:42,620 --> 00:32:48,225
The demo app is not launched and only
the required framework is loaded.

344
00:32:50,780 --> 00:32:54,622
We configure the unit test
targets to run independently.

345
00:32:56,820 --> 00:33:01,235
The Host Application setting is None.

346
00:33:04,180 --> 00:33:08,129
The second operational challenge
is bundle ID and code signing.

347
00:33:10,460 --> 00:33:16,393
Every runnable demo app needs a bundle
ID and the correct signing configuration.

348
00:33:18,740 --> 00:33:26,800
Every reusable demo app needs a bundle
ID and the correct signing configuration.

349
00:33:29,224 --> 00:33:33,794
With automatic signing
enabled, Xcode can create

350
00:33:33,794 --> 00:33:39,370
App ID and provisioning
profile for the bundle ID.

351
00:33:41,080 --> 00:33:46,912
But with hundreds of demo
apps, the developer's

352
00:33:47,815 --> 00:33:50,060
portal fills up with
identifiers and profiles.

353
00:33:52,620 --> 00:33:56,760
Xcode registers a bundle ID
and creates a profile for

354
00:33:56,760 --> 00:33:58,260
every demo app.

355
00:33:58,260 --> 00:34:04,194
This increases maintenance
work when resetting Xcode,

356
00:34:04,194 --> 00:34:07,264
onboarding developers or
registering new test devices.

357
00:34:10,240 --> 00:34:15,041
Xcode also spends time updating
signing information for many targets.

358
00:34:17,800 --> 00:34:23,673
Most of our demo apps are temporary
tools for checking developed results.

359
00:34:24,460 --> 00:34:26,660
Usually, developers

360
00:34:26,660 --> 00:34:30,139
run only one at a time.

361
00:34:30,720 --> 00:34:34,314
So most demo apps can
share the same bundle ID.

362
00:34:35,140 --> 00:34:40,823
We create a unique ID only when
special capabilities require it.

363
00:34:43,980 --> 00:34:48,460
Oh, yeah, almost finished.

364
00:34:48,460 --> 00:34:51,420
Today, I shared many techniques.

365
00:34:51,420 --> 00:34:54,980
Module design, project
generation, dependency injection,

366
00:34:54,980 --> 00:34:58,882
feedback environment, resource
management, and demo apps.

367
00:34:59,560 --> 00:35:05,745
But no single tool or technique
is the most important thing.

368
00:35:07,940 --> 00:35:12,685
On large projects, it is very easy
to lose the overall direction.

369
00:35:13,220 --> 00:35:16,682
Each team can make
reasonable local decisions.

370
00:35:17,360 --> 00:35:24,429
But over time, those decisions may lead
the project in different directions.

371
00:35:25,391 --> 00:35:29,906
So before choosing tools
or rules, we need shared

372
00:35:29,906 --> 00:35:34,474
architectural decisions,
directions, and a clear roadmap.

373
00:35:35,660 --> 00:35:41,878
The roadmap does not need to describe
every detail, but it should show

374
00:35:41,878 --> 00:35:47,482
where the project is now, where we want
to go, and why we are moving there.

375
00:35:48,540 --> 00:35:54,100
We should review it regularly
as the project and team change.

376
00:35:55,257 --> 00:36:00,992
This helps every local decision move
the whole project in the same direction.

377
00:36:03,100 --> 00:36:07,303
Our modularization journey was
not about creating more modules.

378
00:36:08,060 --> 00:36:11,598
It was about creating a
better development experience,

379
00:36:12,527 --> 00:36:16,284
faster feedback, safer changes,
and predictable results.

380
00:36:17,160 --> 00:36:21,333
Through every change, we
must keep that direction.

381
00:36:22,920 --> 00:36:25,348
Thank you for listening.

382
00:36:25,348 --> 00:36:26,360
Thank you.
